  <h2>How to Choose Where to Stay</h2>
  <p>Seoul is massive — the greater metro area is home to over 25 million people. The neighborhood you stay in shapes your entire experience. Stay in the right area and everything is walkable. Stay in the wrong one and you'll spend half your trip on the subway.</p>

  <h2>🛍️ Myeongdong — Best for First-Timers & Shoppers</h2>
  <p>The most tourist-friendly neighborhood in Seoul. Every major K-beauty brand has a flagship here, the streets are alive with street food vendors until midnight, and Lotte/Shinsegae department stores are steps away. Excellent subway access.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Best for:</strong> First-time visitors, K-beauty shoppers, families</li>
    <li><strong>Subway:</strong> Myeongdong Station (Line 4)</li>
    <li><strong>Vibe:</strong> Energetic, commercial, tourist-friendly</li>
    <li><strong>Nearby:</strong> N Seoul Tower, Namdaemun Market, Cheonggyecheon Stream</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>🎵 Hongdae — Best for K-Culture & Nightlife</h2>
  <p>The beating heart of Seoul's youth culture. Independent music, street performances, clubs, and the best late-night food scene. K-POP fans will love it — idol fan cafes, entertainment company offices, and music studios are all in the area. Also excellent transport: direct AREX line to Incheon Airport.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Best for:</strong> Young travelers, K-POP fans, nightlife seekers</li>
    <li><strong>Subway:</strong> Hongik University Station (Line 2 + AREX)</li>
    <li><strong>Vibe:</strong> Youthful, creative, energetic around the clock</li>
    <li><strong>Nearby:</strong> Sinchon, Sangsu, Mangwon Market</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>💎 Gangnam — Best for Luxury & Business</h2>
  <p>Made famous by PSY's global hit, Gangnam is Seoul's upscale district. Tree-lined boulevards, luxury boutiques, Michelin-starred restaurants, and COEX Mall — one of Asia's largest underground shopping centers. More expensive, but worth it for the premium experience.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Best for:</strong> Luxury travelers, business visitors, Michelin dining</li>
    <li><strong>Subway:</strong> Gangnam Station (Line 2)</li>
    <li><strong>Vibe:</strong> Sophisticated, modern, upscale</li>
    <li><strong>Nearby:</strong> Cheongdam, Apgujeong Rodeo, COEX Aquarium</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>🏯 Insadong — Best for Culture & Tradition</h2>
  <p>Where old Seoul lives. Traditional tea houses, antique shops, Korean craft galleries, and hanbok rental shops line winding streets. A beautiful contrast to ultra-modern Seoul. Palaces Gyeongbokgung and Changdeokgung are minutes away.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Best for:</strong> Culture seekers, photographers, couples, history lovers</li>
    <li><strong>Subway:</strong> Anguk Station (Line 3)</li>
    <li><strong>Vibe:</strong> Calm, artsy, traditionally Korean</li>
    <li><strong>Nearby:</strong> Gyeongbokgung Palace, Bukchon Hanok Village, Samcheong-dong</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>🌍 Itaewon — Best for International Travelers</h2>
  <p>Seoul's most cosmopolitan neighborhood. Diverse restaurants from 50+ countries, international fashion, LGBTQ+-friendly venues, and a vibrant bar scene. The adjacent Gyeongridan-gil and Haebangchon (HBC) areas have exploded with trendy cafes and boutiques.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Best for:</strong> International visitors, foodies, LGBTQ+ travelers</li>
    <li><strong>Subway:</strong> Itaewon Station (Line 6)</li>
    <li><strong>Vibe:</strong> Diverse, international, cosmopolitan</li>
    <li><strong>Nearby:</strong> Namsan, War Memorial of Korea, Gyeongridan-gil</li>
  </ul>

